Based on the failing case (dropping a shape onto a straight sequence‐flow), you always end up bailing out here:

```js
    // due to inaccuracy intersection might have been found
    if (!waypointsBefore.length || !waypointsAfter.length) {
      return;
    }
```

In a two‐point sequence flow (start→end) a mid-segment insertion will give you

  – waypointsBefore = []  
  – waypointsAfter  = [ end ]

and so the guard trips and you never split the flow at all.  As a result the reconnect is called with `undefined` dock points and the layout engine docks both ends at the shape’s top-left.

What you really want to do is only abort if **both** sides are empty, not if **either** side is empty.  Change that `||` to `&&` so that a single non-empty side will still let you proceed:

```javascript
### lib/features/modeling/behavior/DropOnFlowBehavior.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
      // due to inaccuracy intersection might have been found
      if (!waypointsBefore.length || !waypointsAfter.length) {
        return;
      }
=======
      // due to inaccuracy intersection might have been found
      // only abort if *both* sides are empty
      if (!waypointsBefore.length && !waypointsAfter.length) {
        return;
      }
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```

With that one‐line change you will now allow the split on simple two‐point flows.  The rest of the logic will then take your computed `waypointsBefore`/`waypointsAfter` (or at least the single non‐empty side) and hand the correct docking information into the reconnection commands, preserving your sequence‐flow layout instead of collapsing everything to the top-left.
